E = {
200: ("What is done when he dies", ["When he dies, his jaw is tied and his eyes are closed, and the one who closes them says: \"In the name of Allah and upon the religion of our master the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. O Allah, make his affair easy for him, make what comes after easy for him, make him happy with meeting You, and make what he has gone to better than what he has left.\" A piece of iron is placed on his stomach so that it does not swell, and his hands are placed at his sides; it is not permitted to place them on his chest. Reciting the Qur'an near him is disliked until he is washed. There is no harm in informing people of his death."]),
201: ("Preparing and washing him", [
 "His preparation is hastened. He is placed as he died on a bed perfumed with incense an odd number of times, in whatever direction happens to suit, according to the soundest view. His 'awrah is covered, then his clothes are removed and he is given wudu, unless he is a young child who did not understand the prayer, without rinsing the mouth and nose unless he was in janabah. Water boiled with lote leaves or potash is poured over him, otherwise plain pure water. His head and beard are washed with marsh mallow. Then he is laid on his left side and washed until the water reaches the part next to the board, then on his right side likewise. Then he is sat up, supported, and his stomach is gently wiped, and whatever comes out is washed away, without repeating (the washing). He is dried with a cloth. Hanut (perfume) is put on his head and beard, and camphor on the places of prostration.",
 "In the apparent narrations, cotton is not used in the washing. His nails and hair are not cut, and his hair and beard are not combed.",
 "A wife may wash her husband, but not the reverse; just as an umm walad does not wash her master.",
 "If a woman dies among men, they give her tayammum, and likewise the reverse, using a cloth; if a mahram relative is present, he gives tayammum without a cloth. Likewise a khuntha whose sex is unclear is given tayammum, according to the apparent narration. A man or woman may wash a young boy or girl who has not reached the age of desire. There is no harm in kissing the deceased.",
]),
202: ("Who prepares him", ["A man must pay for the preparation of his wife, even if he is poor, according to the soundest view. For one who has no wealth, his shroud is on whoever was obliged to support him. If there is no such person, it is from the public treasury; if it does not give, through inability or injustice, it is on the people. Someone who cannot do so himself asks for it for him."]),
203: ("The shroud according to the Shari'ah", [
 "The sunnah shroud for a man is a shirt, a waist-wrapper and a full wrap, of what he used to wear in his life. The sufficient shroud is a waist-wrapper and a full wrap. White cotton is best. The waist-wrapper and full wrap each reach from the head to the feet. His shirt has no sleeves, gusset or collar opening, and its edges are not hemmed. A turban is disliked, according to the soundest view. It is wrapped from his left, then his right, and tied if it is feared it will come open.",
 "In the sunnah shroud, a woman is given in addition a head covering for her face and a cloth to bind her breasts; in the sufficient shroud, a head covering. Her hair is put in two plaits on her chest over the shirt, then the head covering over it beneath the full wrap, then the cloth over that. The shrouds are perfumed with incense an odd number of times before he is wrapped in them. The shroud of necessity is whatever can be found.",
]),
204: ("The funeral prayer: its ruling and pillars", [
 "Praying over him is a communal obligation.",
 "Its pillars are the takbirs and standing.",
]),
205: ("Its conditions are six", ["The deceased being a Muslim; his being purified; his being placed in front of the people; his being present, or most of his body, or half with the head; the one praying not being mounted without an excuse; and the deceased being on the ground. If he is on a mount or on people's hands, the prayer is not permitted, according to the chosen view, except for an excuse."]),
206: ("Its sunnahs are four", ["The imam standing level with the deceased's chest, male or female; the opening praise after the first takbir; sending blessings on our master the Prophet ﷺ after the second; and supplicating for the deceased after the third."]),
207: ("The supplication in the funeral prayer", [
 "Nothing in particular is required for it. If he makes a transmitted supplication, that is better and more effective. Among them is what 'Awf memorised of the supplication of our master the Prophet ﷺ:",
 "\"O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, give him well-being and pardon him. Honour his lodging and make wide",
 "his entrance. Wash him with water, snow and hail, and cleanse him of sins as a white garment is cleansed of dirt. Give him a home better than his home, a family better than his family and a spouse better than his spouse. Admit him to Paradise and protect him from the punishment of the grave and the punishment of the Fire.\"",
 "He gives salam after the fourth without a supplication, according to the apparent narration, and does not raise his hands except at the first takbir. If the imam says the takbir five times he is not followed, but one waits for his salam, according to the chosen view. Forgiveness is not asked for an insane person or a child; instead he says: \"O Allah, make him a forerunner for us, make him a reward and a treasure for us, and make him an intercessor for us whose intercession is accepted.\"",
]),
208: ("Who has most right to lead the funeral prayer", ["The ruler has most right to lead his prayer, then his deputy, then the judge, then the imam of the neighbourhood, then the guardian (next of kin). The one with the right to go forward may permit someone else. If someone else prays, he may repeat it if he wishes, and those who prayed with the other do not repeat it with him. The one with the right to lead it has more right than the person the deceased requested in his will to lead it, according to the position on which fatwa is given. If he is buried without the prayer, it is prayed over his grave, even if he was not washed, as long as the body has not decomposed."]),
209: ("Several funerals together", ["When there are several funerals, praying separately for each is better, and the best is put first, then the next best. If they are gathered and prayed over once, they are put in a long row towards the qiblah so that the chest of each is in front of the imam, keeping the order: men nearest the imam, then boys, then the khuntha, then women. If they are buried in one grave, they are placed in the reverse order."]),
210: ("Following the imam in it", ["One who finds the imam between two takbirs does not join straight away but waits for the imam's takbir and then enters with him, agrees with him in his supplication, then makes up what he missed before the bier is lifted. One who was present at the opening takbir does not wait for the imam's takbir. One who comes after the fourth takbir and before the salam has missed the prayer, according to the sound view."]),
211: ("Where it is prayed", ["It is disliked to pray over him in a congregational masjid, whether he is inside it or outside and some of the people are in the masjid, according to the chosen view."]),
212: ("Praying over infants and children", ["A baby who cries out (on birth) is named, washed and prayed over. If he did not cry out, he is washed, according to the chosen view, wrapped in a cloth and buried, and not prayed over. Likewise a child captured with one of his parents, unless one of them, or he himself, becomes Muslim, or neither parent was captured with him."]),
213: ("Disbelievers and rebels", [
 "If a disbeliever has a Muslim relative, he washes him like washing an impure cloth, shrouds him in a cloth and puts him in a hole, or hands him over to the people of his religion.",
 "No prayer is made over a rebel or highway robber killed while fighting, one who strangled people treacherously, one who attacked with weapons at night in a town, or one killed fighting out of tribal partisanship, even though they are washed.",
]),
214: ("One who kills himself, and one who kills a parent", ["One who killed himself is washed and prayed over; but not one who deliberately killed one of his parents."]),
215: ("Carrying and burying the bier", [
 "It is sunnah for four men to carry it. One should carry it for forty steps, beginning with its front right on one's right shoulder (its right being on the carrier's left), then its back right, then its front left on one's left, then finishing with its back left.",
 "It is recommended to walk quickly with it, but not at a trot, which would shake the deceased.",
 "Walking behind it is better than walking in front of it, as the obligatory prayer is better than the voluntary.",
 "Raising the voice in dhikr, and sitting before it is put down, are disliked.",
]),
216: ("Burying it", [
 "The grave is dug half a man's height, or to the chest, and if more, that is good. A lahd (side niche) is dug, and not a shaqq (trench) except in soft ground. The deceased is brought in from the side of the qiblah, and the one placing him says: \"In the name of Allah and upon the religion of our master the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" He is turned to face the qiblah on his right side, the knots are untied, and unbaked bricks and reeds are set over him; baked bricks and wood are disliked. A woman's grave is covered (with a cloth during burial), not a man's. The earth is poured in, and the grave is raised like a hump, not made square.",
 "Building over it for decoration is haram, and for strength after burial it is disliked. There is no harm in writing on it so that the trace is not lost and it is not disrespected. Burial in houses is disliked, because that is particular to the prophets, upon them be blessings and peace. Burial in vaults is disliked. There is no harm in burying more than one in a grave out of necessity, with earth placed between each two.",
]),
217: ("Death at sea", ["Whoever dies on a ship, when land is far away and harm is feared, is washed, shrouded, prayed over and lowered into the sea."]),
218: ("Travelling with the deceased and moving him", ["It is recommended to bury him in the graveyard of the place where he died or was killed. If he is moved a mile or two before burial, there is no harm, but moving him further is disliked. It is not permitted to move him after burial, by consensus, unless the land was taken unlawfully or taken by pre-emption. If he is buried in a grave dug for someone else, the cost of digging is paid and he is not taken out."]),
219: ("Opening graves", ["A grave is opened for belongings that fell into it, for a stolen shroud, and for wealth buried with the deceased. It is not opened because he was placed not facing the qiblah or on his left side. Allah knows best."]),
220: ("Visiting graves", ["Visiting graves is recommended for men and women, according to the soundest view. It is recommended to recite Ya-Sin, because of what has come that whoever enters the graveyards and recites Ya-Sin, Allah lightens things for them that day, and he has good deeds as many as there are people in it. Sitting on a grave to recite is not disliked, according to the chosen view. Sitting on graves for other than recitation is disliked, as is treading on them, sleeping and relieving oneself on them, and pulling up grass and trees from the graveyard; there is no harm in pulling up what is dry."]),
221: ("Chapter: The rulings of the martyr", ["One who is killed dies at his appointed time, according to us, Ahl al-Sunnah."]),
222: ("Who is a martyr", ["The martyr is one killed by enemy fighters, rebels, highway robbers, or thieves in his home at night, even with a blunt object; or who is found on the battlefield with a wound on him; or who is killed unjustly and deliberately by a Muslim with a sharp weapon; provided he was a Muslim, adult, free of menstruation, nifas and janabah, and did not live on and benefit from life after the fighting ended."]),
223: ("What is done with him", [
 "He is shrouded in his blood and clothes, and prayed over without being washed. What is not suitable as a shroud is removed from him, such as fur, padding, weapons and armour. His clothes may be added to or reduced, but removing them all is disliked.",
 "He is washed if he was killed in janabah, or as a child, insane, in menstruation or nifas, or if he lived on after the fighting ended: by eating, drinking, sleeping, receiving treatment, a prayer time passing while he was conscious, being carried from the battlefield other than for fear of being trampled by horses, making a bequest, buying, selling, or speaking much.",
 "If any of these happened before the fighting ended, he does not count as having lived on.",
 "One killed in a town, when it is not known that he was killed unjustly with an iron weapon, or who was killed by a legal punishment or retaliation, is washed and prayed over.",
]),
224: ("Fasting: its definition", ["It is abstaining during the day from deliberately or mistakenly putting anything into the stomach or what counts as inside, and from sexual desire, with an intention, by one who is eligible for it."]),
225: ("The cause of the obligation of Ramadan", ["The cause of the obligation of Ramadan is witnessing part of it, and each day of it is the cause for fasting that day."]),
226: ("Its ruling and the conditions of its obligation", [
 "It is fard, both on time and made up, for whoever has four things:",
 "Islam, sanity, puberty, and knowing it is obligatory, for one who became Muslim in enemy territory, or being in Muslim lands.",
]),
227: ("Conditions for having to fast it on time", ["The conditions for having to fast it on time are health from illness, menstruation and nifas, and being resident."]),
228: ("Conditions for its valid performance", [
 "Three conditions are required for its performance to be valid:",
 "the intention; being free of what contradicts it, menstruation and nifas; and being free of what invalidates it. Being free of janabah is not a condition.",
]),
229: ("Its pillar", ["Its pillar is refraining from fulfilling the two desires of the stomach and private parts, and what is included with them."]),
230: ("Its ruling", ["Its ruling is the obligation falling from one's responsibility, and reward in the Hereafter."]),
231: ("The kinds of fasting", [
 "Fasting is of six kinds:",
 "fard, wajib, sunnah, recommended, voluntary and disliked.",
 "The fard is the fast of Ramadan, on time and made up, the fast of expiations, and the vowed fast, according to the clearer view.",
 "The wajib is making up a voluntary fast that one broke.",
 "The sunnah is the fast of 'Ashura' together with the ninth.",
 "The recommended is fasting three days of every month, preferably the white days, the thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth; fasting Mondays and Thursdays; fasting six days of Shawwal, and it is said that it is better to fast them in a row and it is said separately; and every fast whose seeking and promised reward are established by the Sunnah, such as the fast of Dawud عليه السلام, who fasted a day and broke his fast a day, which is the best fast and the most beloved to Allah the Exalted.",
 "The voluntary is any other fast not established as disliked.",
 "The disliked is of two kinds: mildly disliked and prohibitively disliked.",
 "The first is fasting 'Ashura' alone without the ninth.",
 "The second is fasting the two 'Ids and the days of tashriq.",
 "It is disliked to single out Friday, or Saturday, or the day of Nawruz or Mihrajan, unless it falls on his usual fast.",
 "It is disliked to fast continuously without breaking (wisal), even for two days, meaning not breaking the fast at all after sunset so that tomorrow's fast joins yesterday's. Fasting every day perpetually is disliked.",
]),
232: ("Fasts that do not need a specified intention", [
 "The kind that does not require specifying the intention or making it at night is fasting Ramadan on time, a vow fixed to a time, and the voluntary fast. These are valid with an intention made from the night until before midday, according to the soundest view; midday here is from dawn to the time of the great forenoon (al-dahwah al-kubra).",
 "It is also valid with an unspecified intention and with the intention of a voluntary fast, even for a traveller or sick person, according to the soundest view. Fasting Ramadan is valid with the intention of another wajib fast for one who is healthy and resident, unlike the traveller, whose fast counts for the wajib he intended. There is a difference over the sick person who intends another wajib in Ramadan.",
 "A vow fixed to a time is not valid with the intention of another wajib; it counts for the wajib he intended.",
]),
233: ("Fasts that need a specified intention", ["The second kind, which requires specifying the intention and making it at night, is making up Ramadan, making up a voluntary fast that one broke, the fasts of all kinds of expiation, and an unrestricted vow, such as saying: \"If Allah cures my sick one, I must fast a day,\" and the cure happens."]),
234: ("How Ramadan is established", [
 "Ramadan is established by sighting its crescent, or by Sha'ban completing thirty days if the crescent is hidden.",
 "The day of doubt is the day after the twenty-ninth of Sha'ban when knowing and not knowing are equal because the crescent was hidden. Every fast is disliked on it, except a voluntary fast intended firmly without wavering between it and another fast; if it turns out to be Ramadan, what he fasted counts for it. If he wavers between fasting and not fasting, he is not fasting. It is disliked to fast one or two days at the end of Sha'ban, but not more than that. The mufti tells the common people to wait on the day of doubt, then to eat when the time for the intention has passed and the matter has not become clear. The mufti, the judge and the elite fast it, meaning those who can keep themselves from wavering in their intention and from thinking of it as the obligatory fast.",
]),
235: ("Sighting the crescent", [
 "Whoever sees the crescent of Ramadan or of al-Fitr alone and his word is rejected must fast; he may not break his fast because he is sure of the crescent of Shawwal. If he breaks his fast in either case he makes it up and owes no expiation, even if he broke it before the judge rejected his word, according to the sound view.",
 "If there is something obscuring the sky, such as cloud or dust, the report of one upright person, or one whose state is unknown, is accepted for Ramadan, according to the sound view, even if he testifies to the testimony of another like him, and even if it is a woman, a slave, or one who was punished for slander and has repented. The words of formal testimony and a claim are not required.",
 "For the crescent of al-Fitr, if the sky is obscured, the words of testimony are required from two free men, or a free man and two free women, without a claim. If the sky is not obscured, a large group is required for Ramadan and al-Fitr, and the size of the large group is left to the imam's judgement, according to the soundest view.",
 "If the thirty days are completed on the testimony of one person and the crescent of al-Fitr is not seen while the sky is clear, breaking the fast is not lawful. There is a difference over the case where it was on the testimony of two upright people. There is no disagreement that breaking the fast is lawful if the sky was obscured, even if Ramadan was established by one person's testimony.",
 "The crescent of al-Adha is like that of al-Fitr.",
 "The other crescents require the testimony of two upright men, or a free man and two free women, who have not been punished for slander.",
]),
236: ("Different horizons", [
 "When it is established on the horizon of one region, it binds all people, according to the apparent position of the madhhab; fatwa and most of the shaykhs are on this.",
 "Sighting the crescent in the daytime does not count, whether before or after midday; it is for the coming night, according to the chosen view.",
]),
237: ("Chapter: What does not break the fast", ["It is twenty-four things: if he eats, drinks or has intercourse forgetfully (if the forgetful person is strong enough to fast, whoever sees him eating reminds him, and not reminding him is disliked; if he is not strong, it is better not to remind him); if he ejaculates from looking or thinking, even if he keeps looking and thinking; if he oils his hair or applies kohl, even if he tastes it in his throat; if he is cupped; backbites; intends to break his fast but does not; if smoke enters his throat without his doing, or dust, even the dust of a mill, or a fly, or the taste of medicine, while he remembers his fast; if he wakes in janabah, even if he stays in janabah all day; if he pours water or oil into his urethra; if he wades into a river and water enters his ear; if he scratches his ear with a stick, dirt comes out on it, and he puts it back in his ear repeatedly; if mucus enters his nose and he deliberately sniffs it in and swallows it (he should spit out phlegm so that his fast is not broken according to the view of Imam al-Shafi'i رحمه الله); if he vomits involuntarily and it goes back down without his doing, even a mouthful, according to the sound view; if he makes himself vomit less than a mouthful, according to the sound view, even if it goes back down, according to the sound view; if he eats what is between his teeth and it is less than a chickpea; or if he chews something the size of a sesame seed from outside his mouth until it disappears and he finds no taste of it in his throat."]),
238: ("Chapter: What breaks the fast and requires expiation with the make-up", ["It is twenty-two things. If one fasting does any of them willingly, deliberately and not compelled, he must make up the fast and pay the expiation: intercourse in either passage, for the one doing it and the one it is done to; eating and drinking, whether of nourishment or medicine; swallowing rain that entered his mouth; eating raw meat, even if it is rotten, unless it has worms; eating fat, in the choice of al-Faqih Abu'l-Layth; dried meat, by agreement; eating wheat or chewing it, unless he chews one grain and it disappears; swallowing a grain of wheat, a sesame seed or the like from outside his mouth, according to the chosen view; eating Armenian clay in any case, and other clay such as tafl if he is used to eating it; a little salt, according to the chosen view; swallowing the saliva of his wife or friend, but not of others; and eating deliberately after backbiting, after cupping, after touching or kissing with desire, after lying with his wife without ejaculation, or after oiling his moustache, thinking that this had broken his fast, unless a jurist gave him that fatwa, or he heard the hadith and did not know its interpretation, according to the madhhab; if he knew its interpretation, the expiation is required. Expiation is required of a woman who willingly complied with one who forced her."]),
239: ("What drops it", [
 "The expiation is dropped if menstruation, nifas or an illness that permits breaking the fast comes on that day.",
 "It is not dropped from one who was forced to travel after it became binding on him, according to the apparent narration.",
]),
240: ("The expiation", ["Freeing a slave, even one who is not a believer. If he is unable to, he fasts two consecutive months with no 'Id day or days of tashriq among them. If he cannot fast, he feeds sixty poor people a satisfying lunch and dinner, or two lunches, or two dinners, or a dinner and a pre-dawn meal; or he gives each poor person half a sa' of wheat or its flour or meal, or a sa' of dates or barley, or its value."]),
241: ("Expiations overlapping", ["One expiation suffices for intercourse and eating repeated over several days, if no expiation was made in between, even from two Ramadans, according to the sound view. If an expiation was made in between, one expiation does not suffice, according to the apparent narration."]),
242: ("Chapter: What breaks the fast without expiation", ["It is fifty-seven things: if one fasting eats raw rice, dough, flour, a large amount of salt at once, non-Armenian clay that he is not used to eating, a date stone, cotton, paper, an unripe and uncooked quince, or a fresh walnut; or swallows a pebble, iron, earth or a stone; or has an enema; or snuffs medicine into his nose; or has something poured into his throat, according to the soundest view; or puts oil or water in his ear, according to the soundest view; or treats a stomach wound or head wound with medicine that reaches his stomach or brain; or rain or snow enters his throat, according to the soundest view, without his swallowing it by his own action; or breaks his fast by mistake, the water of rinsing the mouth running into his stomach; or breaks it under compulsion, even by intercourse; or a woman is forced into intercourse; or a woman, slave or wife, breaks her fast for fear she will become ill from her work; or someone pours water into him while he is asleep; or he eats deliberately after eating forgetfully, even if he knew the hadith, according to the soundest view; or has intercourse forgetfully and then deliberately; or eats after making the intention in the daytime without having made it at night; or begins the day travelling, intends residence, then eats; or travels after beginning the day resident, and eats; or abstains without intending to fast or not to fast; or takes the pre-dawn meal or has intercourse while doubting whether dawn has come, when it had; or breaks the fast thinking the sun has set when it has not; or ejaculates through intercourse with a corpse or an animal, through contact between the thighs or with the stomach, or a kiss or touch; or breaks a fast other than the on-time fast of Ramadan; or a woman is had intercourse with while asleep; or drips something into her private part, according to the soundest view; or he inserts a finger wet with water or oil into his anus, or she into her inner private part, according to the chosen view; or he inserts cotton into his anus until it disappears, or she into her inner private part; or he deliberately draws smoke into his throat; or makes himself vomit, even less than a mouthful, according to the apparent narration, though Abu Yusuf made a mouthful a condition and that is the sound view; or returns vomit that came out involuntarily, when it was a mouthful, while remembering his fast; or eats what is between his teeth when it is the size of a chickpea; or intends to fast during the day after eating forgetfully before making the intention that day; or faints, even for the whole month, though he does not make up the day on which the fainting began or the night before it; or becomes insane, if it does not last the whole month. He does not have to make it up because of becoming resident at night or during the day after the time for the intention has passed, according to the sound view."]),
243: ("Those who must abstain for the rest of the day", ["Whoever's fast was broken must abstain for the rest of the day; likewise a woman whose menstruation or nifas ends after dawn, a boy who reaches puberty, and a disbeliever who accepts Islam after dawn. They must make it up, except the last two."]),
244: ("What is disliked for one fasting", ["Seven things are disliked for one fasting: tasting something and chewing it without an excuse; chewing gum; kissing and intimate touching, if he is not sure he will not ejaculate or have intercourse, according to the apparent narration; collecting saliva in the mouth and then swallowing it; and whatever he thinks will weaken him, such as bloodletting and cupping."]),
245: ("What is not disliked for one fasting", ["Nine things are not disliked for one fasting: kissing and intimate touching when he is sure (of himself); oiling the moustache; kohl; cupping; bloodletting; the siwak at the end of the day, which rather is sunnah as at its start, even if it is fresh or moistened with water; rinsing the mouth and nose other than for wudu; bathing; and wrapping oneself in a wet cloth to cool down, according to the position on which fatwa is given."]),
246: ("What is recommended for one fasting", ["Three things are recommended for him: the pre-dawn meal (suhur), delaying it, and hastening to break the fast, except on a cloudy day."]),
247: ("When breaking the fast is permitted", ["One who fears his illness will increase or be slow to heal may break the fast; likewise a pregnant or nursing woman who fears harm to her mind, death or illness for herself or her child, whether by birth or by nursing. The fear that counts is that based on a strong probability, from experience or from the advice of a skilled, upright Muslim doctor. Likewise one who suffers severe thirst or hunger that he fears will kill him."]),
248: ("Travel", ["The traveller may break the fast, and his fasting is more beloved if it does not harm him and most of his companions are not breaking their fast or sharing their provisions. If they share provisions or are breaking their fast, it is better for him to break his fast in agreement with the group."]),
249: ("Bequests and making up", ["A bequest is not wajib on one who died before his excuse ended, through illness, travel and the like, as mentioned before. They make up what they were able to, as much as their residence and health allowed. Consecutive days are not required in making up. If another Ramadan comes, it is fasted before the make-up, and there is no fidyah for delaying it until then."]),
250: ("The fidyah for the very old", ["Breaking the fast is permitted for a very old man and a very old woman, and they must pay a fidyah of half a sa' of wheat for each day."]),
251: ("A vow to fast forever", ["Likewise one who vowed to fast forever and became too weak because of earning his living breaks the fast and pays the fidyah. If he cannot pay the fidyah because of poverty, he seeks Allah's forgiveness, glorified is He, and asks Him to release him."]),
252: ("Being unable to perform an expiation", ["If he owed the expiation for an oath or for killing and could not find a slave to free, and he is very old, or did not fast until he became very old, the fidyah is not permitted for him, because the fast here is itself a substitute for something else."]),
253: ("The voluntary fast", ["One fasting voluntarily may break his fast without an excuse, according to one narration. Hospitality is an excuse, according to the clearer view, for the guest and the host; and let him take this great benefit as good news."]),
254: ("When one fasting voluntarily must make it up", ["If he breaks it, in whatever way, he must make it up, except if he began a voluntary fast on the five days, the two 'Id days and the days of tashriq; then he does not have to make them up by breaking them, according to the apparent narration."]),
255: ("When a vow must be fulfilled", [
 "If one vows something, he must fulfil it if three conditions are met:",
 "that something of its kind is wajib; that it is intended for itself; and that it is not already wajib. So wudu is not binding by vowing it, nor the prostration of recitation, nor visiting the sick, nor the wajib acts by vowing them.",
 "A vow is valid to free a slave, make i'tikaf, pray a non-obligatory prayer and fast. If he makes an unrestricted vow, or one tied to a condition that comes about, he must fulfil it.",
 "A vow to fast the two 'Ids and the days of tashriq is valid, according to the chosen view; he must not fast them but must make them up. If he fasts them, it counts, though it is haram.",
 "We disregard specifying the time, the place, the dirham and the poor person. So fasting Rajab counts for a vow to fast Sha'ban; praying two rak'ahs in Egypt counts for a vow to pray them in Makkah; giving a dirham in charity counts for a dirham he specified; and giving to Zayd the poor man counts for a vow to give to 'Amr.",
]),
256: ("Fulfilling before the condition", ["If the vow is tied to a condition, what he does before the condition is met does not count for it."]),
257: ("I'tikaf: its definition", ["It is staying, with the intention of it, in a masjid in which the congregation is actually established for the five prayers. So it is not valid in a masjid in which the congregation is not established for the prayers, according to the chosen view. A woman makes i'tikaf in the masjid of her house, which is the place she has set aside for prayer."]),
258: ("The kinds of i'tikaf", ["I'tikaf is of three kinds: wajib, when vowed; an emphasised communal sunnah, in the last ten days of Ramadan; and recommended at other times. Fasting is a condition only for the validity of the vowed i'tikaf. The least voluntary i'tikaf is a short time, even while walking, according to the position on which fatwa is given."]),
259: ("The one in i'tikaf leaving the masjid", ["He does not leave it except for a need of the Shari'ah, such as Jumu'ah; a natural need, such as urinating; or a necessity, such as the masjid collapsing, an oppressor forcing him out, its people dispersing, or fear for himself or his belongings from aggressors, in which case he enters another masjid immediately. If he leaves for a moment without an excuse, the wajib i'tikaf is invalidated and any other ends."]),
260: ("What the one in i'tikaf does", [
 "He eats, drinks, sleeps and makes sales of what he needs for himself or his family in the masjid.",
 "It is disliked to bring the goods into it, to make sales for trade, to keep silent if he believes it to be an act of worship, and to speak except of good.",
 "Intercourse and what leads to it are haram; it is cancelled by intercourse, and by ejaculation from what leads to it.",
]),
261: ("Vowing days and nights", ["Vowing i'tikaf for days binds him to the nights as well, and vowing nights binds him to the days, consecutively, even if he did not stipulate it, according to the apparent narration. Vowing two days binds him to two nights. It is valid to intend the days only, without the nights. If he vows i'tikaf for a month and intends only the days or only the nights, his intention has no effect unless he explicitly makes an exception."]),
262: ("The legitimacy, rank and wisdom of i'tikaf", [
 "I'tikaf is legislated by the Book and the Sunnah, and it is among the noblest deeds when done with sincerity. Among its beauties is that it empties the heart of worldly matters, hands the self over to its Master, keeps one constantly in His worship in His house, and shelters one in His fortress. 'Ata' رحمه الله said: \"The one in i'tikaf is like a man who keeps coming to the door of a great person for a need; the one in i'tikaf says: I will not leave until I am forgiven.\" This is what the weak and lowly one has been able to do, by the care of his Master, the Strong, the Powerful. Praise be to Allah who guided us to this; we would not have been guided had Allah not guided us. And may Allah bless our master and patron Muhammad, the seal of His messengers and prophets, and his family, Companions, descendants and all who follow him.",
 "We ask Allah, glorified is He, to make it purely for His noble Face, to make it of wide benefit, and to make its reward abundant.",
]),
}
